1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,747
We describe a distribution by a few numbers: its mean,

2
00:00:03,847 --> 00:00:07,950
its variance, sometimes its skewness or how heavy its tails

3
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:11,583
are. Each one takes its own calculation. Is there a

4
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:14,789
single object that holds all of them at once?

5
00:00:16,883 --> 00:00:20,329
There is, and it's called the moment-generating function.

6
00:00:20,429 --> 00:00:22,817
The name tells you exactly what it does.

7
00:00:23,916 --> 00:00:26,621
The k-th moment of Y is the expected value of

8
00:00:26,721 --> 00:00:29,176
Y to the k. The first moment is the mean.

9
00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:32,915
The second, together with the first, gives the variance. The

10
00:00:33,015 --> 00:00:36,904
third measures lopsidedness, and there are infinitely many more.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,891
Here is the trick. Define m of t as the expected value of e to

12
00:00:41,991 --> 00:00:46,076
the t Y. At first sight it looks like a strange thing to compute.

13
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:49,681
But expand the exponential as a power series: one, plus t

14
00:00:49,781 --> 00:00:53,107
Y, plus t squared Y squared over two factorial, and so

15
00:00:53,207 --> 00:00:57,293
on. Now take expectations term by term. Every moment appears, each

16
00:00:57,393 --> 00:01:00,718
attached to its own power of t. The moments are packed

17
00:01:00,818 --> 00:01:03,699
into one function, like items on a clothesline.

18
00:01:04,783 --> 00:01:08,144
To get a moment back out, differentiate and set t to

19
00:01:08,244 --> 00:01:12,337
zero. Differentiating once knocks the first term away, and at t

20
00:01:12,437 --> 00:01:17,196
equals zero all higher terms vanish, leaving the mean. Geometrically, the

21
00:01:17,296 --> 00:01:19,259
mean is the slope of m at zero.

22
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:22,939
Differentiate twice for the second moment, and k times for the

23
00:01:23,039 --> 00:01:26,449
k-th. For the outage example from lecture ten, the slope at

24
00:01:26,549 --> 00:01:29,960
zero is point seven five, exactly the mean we found before.

25
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:35,623
There's a second reason MGFs matter. Two distributions with the same MGF

26
00:01:35,723 --> 00:01:39,842
are the same distribution. So an MGF works like a fingerprint: if

27
00:01:39,942 --> 00:01:43,996
you recognise it, you know the distribution. For example, p e to

28
00:01:44,096 --> 00:01:47,306
the t plus q, all to the n, belongs to the binomial

29
00:01:47,406 --> 00:01:51,330
and nothing else. Later in the course, this is how we identify

30
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:54,251
the distribution of sums of random variables.

31
00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:59,908
So the moment-generating function is all the moments on one clothesline.

32
00:02:00,008 --> 00:02:03,725
Differentiate at zero to take one off the line. And because

33
00:02:03,825 --> 00:02:07,736
the line identifies the distribution, it's also a fingerprint.

